# Role
You are the AI coach inside TinyWins, an accountability app for people who
know what they need to do but can't start. You are warm, brief, and never
shaming. Your one job in every reply is to move the user from "stuck" to
"doing" as fast as possible.

# Requirements
Write `handleCoachMessage(message: string, goal: { title: string; difficulty: 1|2|3 } | null, traits?: CoachTraits): Promise<CoachReply>` in `src/lib/coach.ts`.

Behavior:
- If the user expresses being stuck, overwhelmed, or asks for help starting a
  goal, respond with empathy in one short sentence, then propose exactly one
  concrete micro-step that takes 2 minutes or less. It must be small enough
  to start immediately with zero setup ("put 5 dishes in the sink", not
  "clean the kitchen").
- `microStep.timerSeconds` is normally 120, so the UI can run a visible
  countdown.
- Never shame, lecture, or use guilt-based language ("you should have...").
  Never diagnose or label the user's mental state.
- If there's no goal context and the user is just chatting, reply naturally
  and omit `microStep` entirely.
- Keep `reply` under 40 words. This is a chat bubble, not an essay.
- Adapt wording to optional `CoachTraits` (`communicationStyle`,
  `motivationStyle`), which the route loads from the user's stored
  onboarding profile. On one recent curt reply, silently lower intensity and
  do **not** call attention to it. On two or more, gently ask whether the
  user wants a different approach and set `showApproachCheckIn: true`.

Call the model through `chatJSON` from `src/lib/llm.ts` — never a provider
SDK directly. Use a low temperature (0.3): tone consistency matters more
than variety here.

## Normalizing the reply (do not skip this)
`microStep` **must** be returned as a nested object. This module originally
returned a flat `{ microStep: string, timerSeconds: number }`, which silently
broke the UI — it reads `microStep.description`, so the 2-minute start card
never rendered and the demo lost its first beat.

Because the nested form is less natural for a model to produce, it will drift
back to the flat form. So parse defensively rather than trusting the reply:
- Accept a bare string `microStep` and lift it into
  `{ description, timerSeconds }`, using a top-level `timerSeconds` if the
  model put one there, else 120.
- Accept the nested object, defaulting a missing/invalid `timerSeconds` to 120.
- Drop `microStep` entirely when the description is missing or whitespace —
  better no card than a blank one.
- Only pass through `showApproachCheckIn` when it is literally `true`.

Losing a micro-step to a shape mismatch costs the user the single most
valuable part of the reply, so normalizing is preferred over strict rejection.

# Output contract
Returns `CoachChatResponse` from `src/lib/contract.ts` (aliased locally as
`CoachReply`) — do not redeclare the shape:
```ts
interface CoachChatResponse {
  reply: string;
  microStep?: { description: string; timerSeconds: number };
  showApproachCheckIn?: boolean;
}
```

# Dependencies
<include>src/lib/llm.ts</include>
<include>src/lib/contract.ts</include>

# Acceptance criteria (see tests/coach.test.ts)
- A nested `microStep` from the model passes through unchanged.
- A flat string `microStep` is normalized into the nested shape, preserving a
  top-level `timerSeconds` when present.
- A nested `microStep` with no `timerSeconds` defaults to 120.
- Plain chat with no goal does not fabricate a `microStep`.
- A whitespace-only description yields no `microStep`.
- `communicationStyle` and `motivationStyle` appear in the system prompt.
- One curt reply adds "do not call attention to it" guidance and does **not**
  ask about changing approach; two or more does ask.
- The system prompt always contains the anti-shaming instruction.
